WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.334
-Our next guest will be
at The Improv in

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:05.133
Irvine, California,
January 10th.

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:06.934
Making his "Tonight Show" debut,

00:00:06.934 --> 00:00:10.033
please welcome
the very funny Fumi Abe!

00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:12.300
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.234
-Thank you. Thank you so much.

00:00:19.234 --> 00:00:22.334
Thank you. It's good to be here.
My name is Fumi.

00:00:22.334 --> 00:00:25.901
I speak Japanese,
but I grew up in America,

00:00:25.901 --> 00:00:27.234
so the Japanese that I speak,

00:00:27.234 --> 00:00:29.234
I mainly learned it
through textbooks.

00:00:29.234 --> 00:00:30.968
So what that means
is my Japanese --

00:00:30.968 --> 00:00:34.267
it's not bad,
but it's very formal.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.667
And I realized the other day,
it is so formal

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:39.267
that even when I'm on a date
with a Japanese girl

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:41.701
and I'm trying to hook up,

00:00:41.701 --> 00:00:42.968
I just sound like
I'm trying to get her

00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:46.467
to sign
the Treaty of Versailles.

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:47.734
Like, I was out
with this girl recently.

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:49.234
We were dancing at the club,
and she says to me,

00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:52.133
in a very sexy way, "Do you like
the way that feels?"

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:55.167
And what I wanted to say is,
"Yes, it feels amazing,"

00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:59.834
but what came out of my mouth
was, "I derive many pleasures."

00:01:02.868 --> 00:01:05.767
She was like, "Okay,
I guess the war is over.

00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:09.300
I don't know --
I don't know what that means."

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.234
I started comedy
when I was 24 years old,

00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:12.667
and I remember
when I first started,

00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:14.067
my parents were very concerned.

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:16.434
So, I'll never forget this.
I remember they sat me down,

00:01:16.434 --> 00:01:18.200
and they had
a little "talk" with me.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.868
They were like,
"Hey, Fumi, listen,

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:22.267
we think you're making
a huge mistake, alright?

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.567
First of all, you're not even
the funniest person

00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:27.667
in the family, okay?

00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:30.968
Grandma -- way funnier, okay?

00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:32.934
Second of all,
there is no money in comedy.

00:01:32.934 --> 00:01:34.367
How are you going to pay
for stuff in New York?

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:35.734
It's very expensive.

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:37.334
We think you're gonna
look back 10 years from now

00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:39.534
and say to yourself,
'Where did my career go wrong?'"

00:01:39.534 --> 00:01:40.968
And I took that
really personally.

00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:42.400
But then I moved to New York

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.133
and I started making friends
who are my age,

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:47.534
who are musicians, comedians,
artists who were so rich.

00:01:47.534 --> 00:01:49.167
And I realized
they're not rich

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:50.734
because they're famous
or successful.

00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:54.000
They're rich because
their parents are rich, right?

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.501
And their parents
pay for everything.

00:01:56.501 --> 00:01:59.434
Yeah, yeah.
[ Applause ]

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:01.501
Yeah, you guys know
people like that, right?

00:02:01.501 --> 00:02:02.601
Right? Yeah.

00:02:02.601 --> 00:02:03.801
You know those people who, like,

00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:06.033
go get massages
without using a Groupon?

00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:10.267
You what I'm talking about?
These heathens.

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:11.734
And I started making
so many friends like this

00:02:11.734 --> 00:02:13.100
that, one day,
I just had this thought.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.467
I was like, "Dude, if I meet
one more person like that,

00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:18.701
I'm going to have to
sit my parents down

00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:20.767
and have a little talk
with them

00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:23.267
about where their careers
went wrong."

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:25.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:32.834
Because they're looking at me
like, "Why are you so poor?"

00:02:32.834 --> 00:02:35.100
I'm like, "I'm poor
because you're poor.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.667
That is why I'm poor, okay?

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.067
Why don't you go to
law school, okay?

00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:42.200
You go. Why don't you go?"

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.934
One thing about having
immigrant parents

00:02:43.934 --> 00:02:45.501
is that they don't know
a lot about money,

00:02:45.501 --> 00:02:47.467
so they never taught me
how to build wealth.

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.534
So now, as an adult,
whenever I get money,

00:02:49.534 --> 00:02:51.234
I don't know how to invest,
I don't know how to spend it,

00:02:51.234 --> 00:02:52.367
I don't know how
to be rich with it,

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:53.734
and it's kind of embarrassing.

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:55.067
Like, recently,
I did this corporate show

00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:56.267
with my two comedian friends.

00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:57.400
We made a lot of money.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.067
We're talking about
how we spent this money.

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:00.267
And I found out
one of my friends

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:02.200
put a down payment on a Tesla.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.000
The other guy bought
two Rolex watches.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.100
And they looked at me
and they're like,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.033
"Fumi, how did you
spend that money?"

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:09.234
And I was thinking about it.
And I'm not making this up.

00:03:09.234 --> 00:03:11.501
The only thing I started doing
with that money is,

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:16.267
I just started buying, like, the
nicer rice at the grocery store.

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:19.033
Yeah. You know that premium one
that's like 10 more dollars

00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:21.100
than the one you typically buy?

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.300
Isn't that sad?

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.400
Like, literally,
my comedian friends

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.334
are prancing around
in their Gucci loafers,

00:03:26.334 --> 00:03:28.267
and I'm just eating a rice ball
at lunch, being like,

00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:30.501
"Notice anything different?"

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:33.100
[ Applause ]

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.400
I don't know.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.267
I think about money a lot
because I'm a Millennial,

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:43.000
and I feel like our whole thing
is that we work very hard,

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.567
but we don't have a ton of money
in the bank account

00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:46.000
because the economy
keeps messing with us, right?

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.400
Like, let me ask you something.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.534
Clap if you're a Millennial.
Clap if you're a Millennial.

00:03:49.534 --> 00:03:51.534
[ Cheers and applause ]
Okay.

00:03:51.534 --> 00:03:55.100
Now clap if you're a Millennial
and you own a house.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.200
[ Light applause ]
Okay.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.868
Those are all tourists.
Those are all tourists, okay?

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:04.200
They're all from Ohio.
We know that. We know that.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.634
And you know how I know that?

00:04:05.634 --> 00:04:07.167
Because every time I do
this joke in New York or L.A.,

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:09.467
people just start
crying immediately,

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:11.200
and the show is over.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.667
But that makes me sad
that only like three people own.

00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:15.133
That makes me so sad,
because I know

00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:17.133
you guys all work really hard,
and I feel --

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:18.534
That silence that just happened?

00:04:18.534 --> 00:04:21.133
I feel like that's
the reason why, today --

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:22.434
You know how every
young person today

00:04:22.434 --> 00:04:24.868
is trying out,
like, a polyamorous,

00:04:24.868 --> 00:04:28.567
ethically non-monogamous,
open relationship or whatever?

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:30.501
I don't even think we're having
a sexual awakening.

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:33.067
I think we just realize,
as a generation,

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:34.634
that we need
a few more guys to go in

00:04:34.634 --> 00:04:37.834
on a down payment
on a house, right?

00:04:37.834 --> 00:04:40.701
Like, we are not horny, okay?

00:04:40.701 --> 00:04:42.667
It's just that me and my girl --
we don't make enough,

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:46.734
so we have to add
a white guy who loves anime.

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:48.200
You guys have been amazing.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.367
Thank you so much.
I've been Fumi Abe.

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:53.167
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:54.834
-Oh, my God! Well done.

00:04:56.367 --> 00:04:59.667
Fumi! Fumi Abe!

00:04:59.667 --> 00:05:03.133
He'll be at the Irvine Improv
January 10th.

